WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.568 --> 00:00:09.834
JEFF JOHNSON: Hi developers.

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:12.000
My name is Jeff, and I'm a
software engineer working

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.134
on Open Source at Google.

00:00:13.134 --> 00:00:15.550
ALPHA CHEN: I'm Alpha, and I'm
a lab developer at Pivotal.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.190
JEFF JOHNSON: I hope
your Cloud Foundry

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.484
Series is going great so far.

00:00:19.484 --> 00:00:21.900
You and I are going to spend
the next few episodes talking

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.840
about Cloud Foundry
and Stackdriver.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.840
Stackdriver is Google's
suite of logging, monitoring,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.550
and developer tools.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.140
I'm going to be
joined by experts

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.480
who know way more than
I do about Cloud Foundry

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.010
and Stackdriver.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.800
So Alpha, can you give us a
little background about how

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.925
Cloud Foundry is deployed?

00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:40.390
ALPHA CHEN: Sure thing, Jeff.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.630
Cloud Foundry is made
up of many services that

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.060
run on an on-prem
data center, or the VM

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.690
infrastructure of a cloud host,
such as Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.192
These services are deployed as
jobs by BOSH to several VMS,

00:00:52.192 --> 00:00:54.150
and together they form
a working Cloud Foundry.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.660
JEFF JOHNSON: So we've got
all these applications that

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.260
must be doing tons of logging,
in addition to Linux services,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.730
logging to sys log.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.569
How can an operator
make sense of all that?

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.069
ALPHA CHEN: That's
a great question.

00:01:06.069 --> 00:01:09.840
Every BOSH job writes logs to a
known location on the instance.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.550
So an operator can
use the BOSH CLI

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.410
to grab a recent set of logs
or stream them as they come in.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.300
This is fantastic
for initial setup,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.360
but operators really need
to set up a long term

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.330
solution for monitoring
and storing logs.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.680
JEFF JOHNSON: That's why we
work together with Pivotal

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.220
to develop these Stackdriver
tools BOSH release.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.030
This drop-in solution
gives operators

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.020
long term access to this
information in Stackdriver

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.005
logging and monitoring.

00:01:32.005 --> 00:01:33.630
ALPHA CHEN: We work
together in Seattle

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.634
using a Pivotal process.

00:01:34.634 --> 00:01:36.300
We co-located once a
week in our office,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:37.530
and once a week in yours.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.071
And together we developed the
Stackdriver [INAUDIBLE] tooling

00:01:40.071 --> 00:01:42.530
that enables Cloud Foundry
to talk to Stackdriver.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.490
JEFF JOHNSON: We did
this all out in the open

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.250
and it was a lot more fun than
I thought it was going to be.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.690
Now hear me out.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.605
We had a lot of folks
who knew about BOSH,

00:01:50.605 --> 00:01:53.670
a couple of folks who knew
about Cloud Foundry and Go,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.920
a little bit of GCP of course,
but not a lot of overlap.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.640
By pair programming we were
able to spread that knowledge

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.730
across, and we all came out
with a much better understanding

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.440
of each other's products.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.719
So Alpha, can you pair
with me and show me

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:08.760
how to set up Stackdriver
tools on Cloud Foundry?

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:09.801
ALPHA CHEN: Let's get to.

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:12.332
It First, let's upload this
Stackdriver tools release

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.040
to our BOSH director
from our open source

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.730
project hosted on GitHub.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.260
Now we'll have to modify our
deployment manifest to include

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.270
the release.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:21.920
The two jobs,
Google [? Phone D ?]

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.780
and Stackdriver
agent will need to be

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.390
collocated on the instances
that you want monitored.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.150
After that we'll have to
run BOSH deploy as usual.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.800
In recent versions
of BOSH we should

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.000
be able to deploy
this as an add-on.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.050
JEFF JOHNSON: Wow.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.630
That's the fastest BOSH
deploy that's ever happened.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.040
Editing is a real miracle.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.935
It might take a few
minutes on your machine.

00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:43.060
So now that we've got jobs
running across our Cloud

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.210
Foundry, which means our host
logs and metrics should appear

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.830
in Stackdriver,
let's make our data

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.190
little more interesting
by waiting a week or so.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.960
ALPHA CHEN: Wow,
that went by fast.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.020
JEFF JOHNSON: Great.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:53.784
Let's dive in.

00:02:53.784 --> 00:02:55.200
ALPHA CHEN: As the
logs stream in,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.730
we can view and drill
down the specific events.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.560
These logs include all
distance log output

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.330
from the instances
in our deployment,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.340
as well as the logs
from any other BOSH jobs

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.140
on the instance.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.600
Each entry contains contacts,
including the job name,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.760
which allows operators to easily
filter and search through logs.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.160
All of these logs come from
the Google Fluent E job

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.226
that we deployed earlier.

00:03:16.226 --> 00:03:18.140
JEFF JOHNSON: As for
the Stackdriver agent,

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.380
that's responsible for gathering
host metrics with Collect D

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.616
and sending them back to
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:03:23.616 --> 00:03:25.490
I'm going to hop into
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.781
and we can now see detailed
information about our Cloud

00:03:27.781 --> 00:03:30.910
Foundry infrastructure.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.450
Let's take a look at the
dashboard I created earlier.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.120
The dashboard shows metrics
gathered by the agent.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.770
Now we can look
for abnormalities

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.900
and set up a learning threshold,
such as high memory usage.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.390
ALPHA CHEN: This
is a great start

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.270
to having a single pane of
glass for a CF operator.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.710
These deployments are made up
of tons of virtual machines.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.710
So having a heartbeat
like this is a great thing

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.226
to keep an eye on.

00:03:51.226 --> 00:03:54.200
JEFF JOHNSON: Let's look at
the log metrics we've created.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.840
This metric is based off
of an event appearing

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.880
in the Stackdriver logs.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.260
We can drill into the actual
log events where this occurred.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.330
This gives us a query in
the Stackdriver logging

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.546
console specific to our
event and time range.

00:04:08.546 --> 00:04:10.670
I'm going to filter to our
specific virtual machine

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.550
and remove the event filter
to gain some context.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.070
This is an incredibly
useful tool

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.079
for keeping an eye
on your entire system

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:20.360
and drilling down when
something pops up.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.072
That's host monitoring
with Stackdriver.

00:04:22.072 --> 00:04:23.780
ALPHA CHEN: And check
out the description

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.100
for more resources
on integrating

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.398
Cloud Foundry with Stackdriver.

00:04:26.398 --> 00:04:29.604
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:35.201 --> 00:04:37.400
JEFF JOHNSON: Thank
you all for watching.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.800
Check out some more videos
we've done about Stackdriver

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.990
and Cloud Foundry, and
check out our play list

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.730
for even more information.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.610
Make sure to subscribe to
the Google Cloud channel

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:47.937
right here.

